Presidential elections were held in [[North Macedonia|Macedonia]] in 2009. The first round was held on 22 March, alongside [[2009 Macedonian local elections|local elections]]. As no candidate received more than 50% of the vote, a run-off round was held on 5 April 2009,<ref>[http://www.mia.com.mk/default.aspx?vId=60659974&lId=2 Macedonian Information Agency] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110727214125/http://www.mia.com.mk/default.aspx?vId=60659974&lId=2 |date=27 July 2011 }}</ref> which was won by [[Gjorge Ivanov]] of the center-right [[VMRO-DPMNE]] party. Incumbent [[President of the Republic of Macedonia|President]] [[Branko Crvenkovski]] did not stand for re-election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.a1.com.mk/vesti/default.aspx?VestID=95350|title=Црвенковски: Нема да се кандидирам на претседателските избори|publisher=A1 Televizija|date=26 May 2008|access-date=27 May 2008|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20080717113604/http://www.a1.com.mk/vesti/default.aspx?VestID=95350|archivedate=17 July 2008}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The elections were held using the [[two-round system]]; a candidate required a majority of the vote in the first round of voting, with a second round held if no candidate crossed the threshold. There was also a requirement for voter turnout in the second round to be at least 40% to validate the result.<ref>[http://www.mia.com.mk/default.aspx?vId=63512227&lId=2&pmId=501 Macedonian Information Agency] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110727214807/http://www.mia.com.mk/default.aspx?vId=63512227&lId=2&pmId=501 |date=27 July 2011 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.setimes.com/cocoon/setimes/xhtml/en_GB/newsbriefs/setimes/newsbriefs/2008/10/08/nb-07 |title=Macedonia to hold presidential, local elections on March 15th |publisher=SETimes.com |date=15 March 2009 |accessdate=6 April 2009}}</ref>

==Candidates==
On 25 January 2009, the largest party in the [[Assembly of the Republic of Macedonia|Macedonian parliament]], VMRO-DPMNE, appointed [[Gjorge Ivanov]] as the party's presidential candidate.<ref>[http://www.mia.com.mk/default.aspx?vId=61007134&lId=2 Macedonian Information Agency] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120305020531/http://www.mia.com.mk/default.aspx?vId=61007134&lId=2 |date=5 March 2012 }}</ref> On 26 January 2009, the biggest opposition party in the country, the Social Democratic Union of Macedonia, proposed the former Minister of Internal Affairs and former Minister of Foreign Affairs [[Ljubomir Frčkoski]] for presidential candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.setimes.com/cocoon/setimes/xhtml/en_GB/newsbriefs/setimes/newsbriefs/2009/01/27/nb-04 |title=Macedonia's opposition SDSM announces presidential candidate |publisher=SETimes.com |date=27 January 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009}}</ref> The other candidates were the [[independent (politician)|independent]] [[Ljube Boškoski]], [[New Democracy (Macedonia)|New Democracy]] leader Imer Selmani, [[Nano Ružin]] from the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Republic of Macedonia)|Liberal Democratic Party]], Agron Buxhaku of the [[Democratic Union for Integration]] and Mirushe Hoxha of the [[Democratic Party of Albanians]], the only female candidate.

==Opinion polls==
There was a poll held in January 2009, before any names of candidates for president were made official, according to which 31.2% of the Macedonian citizens would vote for the candidate to be proposed by conservative party [[VMRO-DPMNE]] while 11.4% would give their vote to the candidate of the [[Social Democratic Union of Macedonia]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/32643/vmro_dpmne_candidate_first_for_macedonians |title=VMRO-DPMNE Candidate First for Macedonians: Angus Reid Global Monitor |publisher=Angus-reid.com |date=18 January 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009 |url-status=usurped |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090119064911/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/32643/vmro_dpmne_candidate_first_for_macedonians |archivedate=19 January 2009}}</ref>

A poll from February 2009 saw Ivanov at 27%, Frčkoski at 13%, Boškoski at 10%, Selmani at 9% and other candidates together at 18%. In a run-off, Ivanov would win over Frčkoski with 36% to 21%, with a large number of undecideds, however.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/32896/ivanov_leads_in_macedonian_presidential_race |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120630030708/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/32896/ivanov_leads_in_macedonian_presidential_race |url-status=usurped |archive-date=30 June 2012 |title=Ivanov Leads in Macedonian Presidential Race: Angus Reid Global Monitor |publisher=Angus-reid.com |date=2 March 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009 }}</ref> A poll from shortly before the election saw Ivanov leading with 23.1% before Selmani with 13.3% and Frčkoski at 9.7%; 23.9% were undecided.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/33043/ivanov_ahead_in_macedonian_election |title=Ivanov Ahead in Macedonian Election: Angus Reid Global Monitor |publisher=Angus-reid.com |access-date=6 April 2009 |url-status=usurped |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090322031951/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/33043/ivanov_ahead_in_macedonian_election |archivedate=22 March 2009}}</ref>

A new poll, that was held one week prior to the run-off, saw Ivanov in lead with 25.4% and Frčkoski at 13.8% (that is, 69% to 31% of decided voters).<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.vecer.com.mk/?ItemID=E2A494BB76A59A47A3CA76D24C5ED5FD |title=Večer Online |access-date=30 March 2009 |archive-date=2 April 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090402110331/http://vecer.com.mk/?ItemID=E2A494BB76A59A47A3CA76D24C5ED5FD |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/33118/ivanov_could_secure_victory_in_macedonia |title=Ivanov Could Secure Victory in Macedonia: Angus Reid Global Monitor |publisher=Angus-reid.com |date=1 April 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090402074216/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/33118/ivanov_could_secure_victory_in_macedonia |archive-date=2 April 2009 |url-status=usurped}}</ref>

==Conduct==
Nearly 7,000 Macedonian and 500 foreign observers monitored the vote at Macedonia's nearly 3,000 polling stations.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB123772225090905823 |title=Macedonia Holds Key Vote |newspaper=The Wall Street Journal |date=23 March 2009 |access-date=23 March 2009 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090324083134/http://online.wsj.com/article/SB123772225090905823.html |archivedate=24 March 2009 }}</ref> A fair and democratic election has been seen as an important factor for Macedonia's induction to [[NATO]] and the [[European Union]].

==Results==
In the first round, Ivanov strongly led in front of the other candidates with 35%, with Frčkoski getting 20% and coming in second place and advancing to the run-off. [[New Democracy (Macedonia)|New Democracy's]] Imer Selmani, who had been dubbed the "Macedonian Obama" for his ability to transcend the ethnic boundaries and appeal to ethnically Macedonian voters, as well, was by far the most successful candidate, gaining 15% of the vote and coming in third place, closely behind the independent Boškoski.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://edition.cnn.com/2009/WORLD/europe/03/23/macedonia.election/ |title=Macedonia vote 'free of violence' |publisher=CNN  |date=23 March 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author= |url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5iqlUMPZzuZK1HV6o0I7NjSpnwq1Q |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130102235814/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5iqlUMPZzuZK1HV6o0I7NjSpnwq1Q |url-status=dead |archive-date=2 January 2013 |title=AFP: Macedonia holds polls crucial for EU bid |publisher=Google  |date=21 March 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009}}</ref> 103 voting stations could not open due to heavy snowfall; voting was to be rescheduled there.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.euractiv.com/en/enlargement/quiet-vote-comforts-macedonia-eu-bid/article-180541 |title=Quiet vote comforts Macedonia's EU bid &#124; EU – European Information on Enlargement & Neighbours |publisher=EurActiv.com |date=23 March 2009 |access-date=6 April 2009 |archive-date=23 November 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091123154335/http://euractiv.com/en/enlargement/quiet-vote-comforts-macedonia-eu-bid/article-180541 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Some days before the second round of the elections took place, VMRO-DPMNE and the Democratic Union for Integration agreed on supporting each other on both, the local and presidential elections.<ref>[http://www.makfax.com.mk/en-Us/Details.aspx?itemID=2512 Makfax News Agency]</ref>

In part due to the existence of many voters in the registers who have left Macedonia years ago and live abroad, and in part due to the lack of motivation for ethnic Albanians to participate in the second round, there were fears that the minimum turnout of 40% might not be met in the second round, invalidating the election.<ref>{{cite web|author=derStandard.at GmbH |url=http://derstandard.at/?url=/?id=1237228795589 |title=derStandard.at |publisher=derStandard.at |access-date=6 April 2009}}</ref>

There was a significant number of invalid ballots in both rounds, 3.15% in the first round and even 5.87% in the runoff. According to media reports, this was due to voter apathy and disenchantment with the candidates. It is reported that there was a Facebook group "Vote for Chuck Norris" with thousands of fans.<ref>[http://www.janes.com/articles/Foreign-Report-2009/Macedonian-voters-search-for-a-hero.html Jane's Foreign Report: Macedonian voters search for a hero]</ref> It was also reported that on thousands of ballots names like "Chuck Norris", "GOD", "Jimi Hendrix" or "George Bush" were hand-written by the voters.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.utrinski.com.mk/default.asp?ItemID=05CB7FE8C3DCD3438EFB82F2C767C8D9 |title=Utrinski Vesnik: Chuck Norris eight presidential candidate (Macedonian, with photos) |access-date=8 April 2009 |archive-date=14 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110614103311/http://www.utrinski.com.mk/default.asp?ItemID=05CB7FE8C3DCD3438EFB82F2C767C8D9 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://macedoniaonline.eu/content/view/6107/1/ |title=Macedonians affirm their sense for humour during elections... |access-date=8 April 2009 |archive-date=14 April 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090414131758/http://macedoniaonline.eu/content/view/6107/1 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

